THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
The higher-order attack graph proposal restricts attack order to at most 2 and defines a monotone operator on the extended graph, but it does not establish that reinstatement remains a derived property under this extended semantics. The problem requires that reinstatement be preserved as a derived property, and the proposal itself acknowledges this risk ('may break the derived property of reinstatement unless the operator is carefully designed'), yet it provides no proof or construction showing that, with order-2 attack-nodes and the chosen monotone operator, a node is reinstated exactly when all its attackers are attacked. Without that derivation, the extended semantics may admit cases where a node's attackers are all attacked but the node is not reinstated, or vice versa, so the derived-property guarantee is not shown to be preserved. Counterexample: let A attack B, and let C attack the attack-edge A→B (an order-2 attack-node). Under the extended fixpoint, if C is accepted then A→B is attacked/defeated, so A no longer attacks B; but the proposal gives no rule guaranteeing B is reinstated in the fixpoint, because reinstatement is defined only over original nodes and the operator is not shown to propagate attack-edge defeat back to node reinstatement.

TARGET:
Introduce a higher-order attack graph where attack edges are reified as nodes, and the skeptical fixpoint is extended to these attack-nodes. This allows attacks on attacks, enabling a node to be reinstated when all its attackers are themselves attacked. To preserve determinism and polynomial cost, we restrict the order to at most 2 and define a monotone operator on the extended graph. Criticism: this may break the derived property of reinstatement unless the operator is carefully designed; also the graph size grows quadratically, potentially affecting polynomial bounds.